#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# Run from the `Root>` prompt after scene_demo.etcs, handing it the RIDs the
# navigator shows you:
#
#     Root> RenderProvider                       # read the names + RIDs
#     Root> scripts/scene_layer.etcs view=<RID> backdrop=<RID>
#
# WHY INJECTION AND NOT attach. A script started from the prompt is a ROOT
# script, and preflight scopes a root script against NO globals on purpose --
# a root is the thing that CREATES the globals, so resolving its own names
# against a previous root's would make two independent trees silently share a
# namespace. The refusal is correct; it just means the reaching-in has to be
# explicit, which is what an injected RID is. `requires` below is the
# receiving half of that: it states what this script must be handed, and the
# family it must belong to, and is checked before line one runs.
requires view     [Surface]
requires backdrop [Pixels]

# A new layer, spawned into the live runtime.
spawn RenderProvider::ImageSurface note
note.Create(220, 120)
note.Clear(0.05, 0.35, 0.20, 0.90)
note.DrawRect(10, 10, 200, 30, 0.95, 0.95, 0.90, 1.0)

# WHY THE WHOLE SCENE IS RESTATED, and not just the new layer.
#
# A surface's composition is RETAINED until it is presented, and the first
# draw call after a present starts a NEW one (VulkanSurface::
# beginCompositionIfPresented). So a lone Blit here would not add a layer to
# the picture -- it would REPLACE the picture with one layer. An edit batch is
# a whole composition, always.
#
# That is the honest shape of a scene that lives in a script rather than in
# the entity tree, and it is the clearest argument for the Drawable families:
# once the scene IS the tree, adding a layer is a spawn under a parent and
# DrawInto replays the tree, with nothing to restate.
view.Clear(0.03, 0.03, 0.05, 1.0)
view.Blit(@backdrop, 80, 60, 864, 648, 1.0)
view.Blit(@note, 620, 480, 0, 0, 1.0)
